From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Darren Shepherd <darren@obot.ai>
Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 00:21:42 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] renderer: moving the cursor invalidates the rows it leaves
 and enters

A ligature under the cursor kept the shaping it was given while the
cursor was somewhere else, so the cell beside the cursor blinked with it.

Shaping runs are broken at the cursor (font/shaper/run.zig) so a ligature
under it is split into its components. That break is only applied when a
row is re-shaped, and a row is only re-shaped when it is dirty - which
cursor movement alone never makes it: Screen.cursorLeft/Right/Up/Down and
cursorAbsolute mark nothing.

So with the cursor moved onto the first dash of `--`, the row kept the
ligature it had been shaped with earlier: one glyph, owned by the first
cell, whose ink spans two. Drawing the block cursor over that cell took
the second dash with it, and the dash appeared to blink in antiphase with
the cursor.

Measured, cursor parked on the first dash of `--`, per blink:

  before   365 px across two cells (the neighbour's ink rows included)
  after    338 px - one 13x26 cell, the cursor block and nothing else

Only on an actual move. Dirtying unconditionally would re-shape the
cursor's row every frame, including the ~1.7 frames per second the blink
timer already costs an idle pane - trading a rendering bug for a
performance one.

This is upstream code and upstream has no cursor-move invalidation
either, so the defect should reproduce there; that is inference, not a
measurement, and is recorded as such in KD-06. Verified here against the
D3D11 renderer only, though generic.zig is shared with Metal and OpenGL.
---
 src/renderer/generic.zig | 36 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 36 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/renderer/generic.zig b/src/renderer/generic.zig
index ae5272f87..69b0e5cc8 100644
--- a/src/renderer/generic.zig
+++ b/src/renderer/generic.zig
@@ -154,6 +154,10 @@ pub fn Renderer(comptime GraphicsAPI: type) type {
         /// cells for the draw call.
         cells_rebuilt: bool = false,
 
+        /// Where the cursor was when we last rebuilt cells, so that moving it
+        /// can invalidate the rows it left and arrived on. See rebuildCells.
+        last_cursor_vp: ?terminal.RenderState.Cursor.Viewport = null,
+
         /// The current GPU uniform values.
         uniforms: shaderpkg.Uniforms,
 
@@ -2395,6 +2399,38 @@ pub fn Renderer(comptime GraphicsAPI: type) type {
                 };
             } else null;
 
+            // Moving the cursor has to re-shape the rows it left and arrived
+            // on, even though no cell contents changed.
+            //
+            // Shaping runs are broken at the cursor (font/shaper/run.zig) so a
+            // ligature under it is split into its components. That break is
+            // only applied when a row is re-shaped, and a row is only re-shaped
+            // when it is dirty - which cursor movement alone never makes it.
+            // The row therefore keeps whatever shaping it had when the cursor
+            // was somewhere else.
+            //
+            // The visible cost was KD-06: with the cursor moved onto the first
+            // dash of a `--` ligature, the row kept the ligature it was shaped
+            // with earlier - one glyph, owned by the first cell, whose ink
+            // spans two - so drawing the block cursor over that cell took the
+            // second dash with it, and the dash appeared to blink in antiphase
+            // with the cursor.
+            //
+            // Only on an actual move. Dirtying unconditionally would re-shape
+            // the cursor's row on every frame, including the ~1.7 frames per
+            // second the blink timer already costs an idle pane.
+            cursor_dirty: {
+                const vp = state.cursor.viewport;
+                if (std.meta.eql(self.last_cursor_vp, vp)) break :cursor_dirty;
+                if (self.last_cursor_vp) |prev| {
+                    if (prev.y < row_len) row_dirty[prev.y] = true;
+                }
+                if (vp) |now| {
+                    if (now.y < row_len) row_dirty[now.y] = true;
+                }
+                self.last_cursor_vp = vp;
+            }
+
             for (
                 0..,
                 row_raws[0..row_len],
